# A shell script must reach a POSIX host with LF endings, whatever the checkout does.
#
# Found the expensive way: `benchmarks/harness/run-programme.sh` ran fine when it was first written on this
# machine, and then failed with `set: Illegal option -` the moment a `git checkout` re-materialised it. The
# working tree here is CRLF (core.autocrlf), a `sh` on Linux reads `set -e\r` as an option called `-\r`, and
# the script dies on its first line — inside a container, where the error is one line with no context.
#
# It is not only this file. Every `.sh` in this repository is run by a container or a CI step on Linux, so any
# of them would break the same way as soon as somebody checks the repository out on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf
